 test the Slurm command without the rest of the application:

from slurm_analytics.sacct import (
    SacctConfig,
    build_sacct_command,
    run_sacct,
)

config = SacctConfig(
    start="2026-09-29",
    end="2026-09-30",
)

print(
    " ".join(
        build_sacct_command(config)
    )
)

output = run_sacct(config)

print(output)





from slurm_analytics.pipeline import (
    ingest_sacct,
)

from slurm_analytics.sacct import (
    SacctConfig,
)

from slurm_analytics.pipeline import ingest_sacct


config = SacctConfig(
    start="2026-09-29",
    end="2026-09-30",
)

result = ingest_sacct(
    config
)

print(
    result.consolidated_jobs
)

print(
    result.steps
)



print(result.raw.columns)




print(
    result.raw.select([
        "jobid",
        "jobidraw",
        "tresusageinave",
    ]).head(20)
)






CLI report command
------------------
The package exposes a ``slurm-analytics`` console script when installed.
In a source checkout/container, the same command is also available as:

    python -m slurm_analytics.cli report accounts --output accounts.yaml

To make the exact ``slurm-analytics report ...`` command available:

    python -m pip install -e /workspace

Or add ``/workspace`` to PATH to use the bundled ``/workspace/slurm-analytics`` wrapper.


Import multiple historical sacct files
--------------------------------------
The initialize_slurm command accepts multiple files in one invocation. If the
files were created by collect_sacct.py, keep the adjacent .out.json metadata
sidecars so the importer can map each file's recorded --format fields safely.
For example, in the application container:

    python manage.py initialize_slurm --history-file /data/part-1.out /data/part-2.out --no-accounts

Shell wildcards also work when expanded by the shell, for example:

    python manage.py initialize_slurm --history-file /data/*.out --no-accounts

Files may overlap; the database ingestion uses record-key upserts. The
--start/--end options mark initialization metadata but do not filter records
inside supplied history files.

Historical import diagnostics: `python manage.py initialize_slurm --history-file "rawdata/*.out"` prints compact warnings for rows with unexpected field counts. Add `--verbose` to print raw suspicious rows (up to 50 per file) for troubleshooting.
